    <!-- ============================================================
         FAQ
         ============================================================ -->
    <section id="faq">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="faq">
          <div class="faq-grid">
            <div class="left">
              <span class="mono">FAQ</span>
              <h3>Common questions</h3>
              <p class="faq-blurb">Quick answers to the questions sensible people ask before they download.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-list">
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>What is this?</h4>
                <p>An open-source traffic speed monitor. Plug a radar sensor into a Raspberry Pi, let it record how fast vehicles pass, and it produces a PDF with speed distributions, hourly patterns, and percentile breakdowns. No cloud, no cameras, and no private surveillance habit.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Who is this for?</h4>
                <p>You, probably. If traffic on your street moves faster than it should, this is for you. Parent associations, community groups, neighbourhood watch, and residents with a safety concern and nowhere sensible to take it. If you've ever been told to come back with evidence, this is how you get it.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>What does it measure?</h4>
                <p>Vehicle speeds. A radar sensor records how fast vehicles pass and stores the data on a Raspberry Pi. No faces, no licence plates, no images of any kind.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Where does the data go?</h4>
                <p>Nowhere. It stays on your device, in a local database. There is no cloud account, no upload step, nothing that sends data anywhere without your knowledge. The only way data leaves your network is when you hand (or email) someone the finished PDF.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>What about LiDAR?</h4>
                <p>LiDAR is research-stage work. The pipeline is open, the maths is published, and the current release is a macOS visualiser for engineers and curious users. Today, the most mature path is radar.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>How do I get started?</h4>
                <p>Follow the <a href="/guides/setup/" class="inline-link">setup guide</a>. You need a Raspberry Pi and an OmniPreSense radar sensor. By the end, you'll have a working speed monitor and a PDF report that holds up in a council meeting.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

